{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>And the garments of service (בִּגְדֵי הַשְּׂרָד)</b> I do not know why He did not command at the outset to make these garments, as He did with all the work of the Tabernacle and with the garments of Aaron and his sons. For it would have been fitting that He say to him: And you shall make a garment entirely of tekhelet to cover the ark with it, and a garment of argaman to cover the altar with it, and a garment of tola'at shani to cover the table with it, and now, when He commands the makers, He would include them with the rest of the work. Perhaps there was no strict specification concerning them, and they would make the garments of service as they pleased from one kind alone, and they made them from tekhelet and argaman and tola'at shani. And they did not want the shesh, for it is not as prestigious as they are. And after they were made thus, HaShem wanted that the tekhelet should be for the ark, and the argaman for the altar, and the shani for the table (Numbers 4:6–13). Therefore He furnished them the commandment in brief when He commanded concerning the makers, for according to their own understanding they would make the garments of service.\n\nIt is not fitting that the covering garment should be made hole after hole, according to the words of Rashi (Rashi on Exodus 31:10), but rather it is from the language of שָׂרִיד, because they are of one kind, according to the words of R. Abraham Ibn Ezra. And I have written this matter according to the method of Rashi, and all of this does not sit well with me at all. For what reason would HaShem say to Moses: And they shall make the garments of the curtains, or the garments of שְׂרִידָה, without explaining to him how many there should be — two or a hundred — and how long they should be and how wide, and why they should make them? The matter is not understood at all from this statement. Furthermore, that He mentions them always before the garments of Aaron. Furthermore, what is the meaning of \"to serve in the holy\" (Exodus 39:1)? For this would hint at the service of the offerings in the holy, as He says, \"or when they approach the altar to serve in the holy\" (Exodus 28:43), and likewise \"who comes to the Tent of Meeting to serve in the holy\" (Exodus 29:30) — not at the service of the outside and of carrying. Furthermore, for it is written: \"And they shall make all that I have commanded you (Exodus 31:6) — the Tent of Meeting, etc. — and the garments of service (Exodus 31:7–10) — until \"according to all that I have commanded you they shall make\" (Exodus 31:11), and He never commanded him at the outset concerning garments of service at all.\n\nAnd what emerges from the words of our Sages is that the garments of service (בִּגְדֵי הַשְּׂרָד) are the priestly garments themselves, and this is mentioned in tractate Yoma (Yoma 72a). If so, the correct interpretation is that we should explicate בִּגְדֵי הַשְּׂרָד as בִּגְדֵי הַיִּחוּד — the garments of singularity — that is to say, garments in which one of the people, the great one among his brothers, would be singled out by wearing them. And likewise: \"and among the survivors (וּבַשְּׂרִידִים) whom YHWH calls\" (Joel 3:5); \"the survivors (הַשְּׂרִידִים) survived from them\" (Joshua 10:20) — from the expression \"one who escapes and survives (פָּלִיט וְשָׂרִיד)\" (Jeremiah 44:14). And it hints at the garments of Aaron, and He calls them thus because only a singular individual in his generation may wear them — Aaron during his lifetime, and after him the High Priest upon whom the anointing oil is poured on his head and whose hand is filled to put on the garments (Leviticus 21:10). And Scripture always mentions the garments of Aaron in a manner of honor, as He says: \"for honor and for splendor\" (Exodus 28:2), \"holy garments\" (Exodus 28:4), \"the golden plate, the holy diadem\" (Leviticus 8:9). Therefore He called them garments of שְׂרָד — garments of royalty.\n\nAnd this is the meaning of \"the garments of service, to serve in the holy, the holy garments for Aaron the priest\" (Exodus 35:19) — for it returns to explain. And likewise it says in the portion of \"And they brought the Tabernacle\" (Exodus 39:1).\n\nAnd what is said here, in the portion of \"See, I have called\" — <b>and the garments of service and the holy garments for Aaron the priest</b> — its meaning is that they are garments of two ranks: garments of שְׂרָד and holy garments, to indicate that only one of the people may wear them when he comes to serve in the holy. And since it did not mention here \"to serve in the holy,\" He added to explain that they are garments of שְׂרָד and garments of holy. Furthermore, several extra vav's have come in the verse.\n\nAnd the meaning of \"And from the tekhelet and the argaman and the tola'at shani they made garments of service, to serve in the holy\" (Exodus 39:1) — since this passage explains these accounts of the Tabernacle, and gives a reckoning of the gold and the silver and the copper: how much Israel brought, and what they made with them, because of their importance. And afterwards it says that from the tekhelet and the argaman and the tola'at shani that had been brought to them, they made the garments of שְׂרָד — he did not add or subtract. And it did not mention the shesh because it is not significant. Perhaps they brought a great amount of it and it was left over. And it returns to explain: \"And they made the holy garments that are for Aaron, as YHWH commanded Moses; and he made the ephod of gold, etc.\" (Exodus 39:1)."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 31, :notes {0 "The abbreviation ר\"א is resolved as R. Abraham Ibn Ezra, whom Ramban cites by this short form elsewhere. The Talmud citation יומא ע\"ב is resolved as Yoma 72a based on standard reference and context."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:19:44.808425618Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}